Consider the following C declaration:<br><pre><code> struct { 
    short x[5];  
    union { 
        float y; 
        long z; 
    } u;
 } t;</code></pre>Assume that the objects of the type short, float and long occupy 2 bytes, 4 bytes and 8 bytes, respectively. The memory requirement for variable t, ignoring alignment consideration, is:

The output of the following program is<br><pre><code> main()
{
    static int x[] = {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8};
    int i;
    for (i=2; i<6; ++i)
    x[x[i]]=x[i];
    for (i=0; i<8; ++i)
    printf("%d", x[i]);
}</code></pre>
